6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation remains divided between Camp A (~3.65–3.66 mL) and Camp B (3.51 mL), hinging on whether the 8 mCi order must be decay-corrected to the 8:00 AM injection time or drawn unadjusted at the 4:04 AM compounding time. The decisive factor remains the mathematical alignment: the 56-minute offset between 4:04 AM, 8:00 AM, and the 12:00 PM calibration cancels out to yield an exact 76.0-hour net decay interval. Because nuclear pharmacy regulations require patient doses to meet the prescribed activity at scheduled administration, the required volume is determined across this 76-hour interval, confirming ~3.65–3.66 mL as the intended and clinically standard answer.
}

11.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation remains divided between adjusting the drawn activity for administration time (3.65–3.66 mL) versus compounding time alone (3.51 mL). Radiopharmaceutical orders require delivering the prescribed dose at the scheduled time of administration, meaning the dose must account for decay between compounding at 4:04 AM and injection at 8:00 AM. Because the physical decay rate is identical in the vial and the syringe, the intermediate time cancels out, yielding an exact 76.0-hour decay interval to the calibration time and confirming the required draw volume is 3.65–3.66 mL.
}

